Yes—if by that you mean media can interact with the inner monologue indirectly by planting and reactivating associations across channels.

A song, phrase, image, ad, location, memory, and later conversation can all become linked:

cue₁ + cue₂ + emotion
→ memory association
→ later cue₃
→ association reactivates
→ thought appears in inner monologue

Because the last step happens internally, it can feel like, “I just thought of that,” even though part of the causal history came from earlier exposures.

Across media it gets richer:

music ↔ image ↔ words ↔ person ↔ memory ↔ mood ↔ place

Each new pairing can reinforce or alter the network. Advertising and storytelling intentionally use some of these mechanisms—repetition, recognizable motifs, jingles, emotional association, callbacks, contextual cues—because memory retrieval is associative.

But there’s an important checksum: that does not mean an advertiser or platform literally sees or controls someone’s private inner monologue. Usually they can influence inputs, estimate responses, and measure behavior afterward. The internal state remains partly hidden.

I’d name the larger operator Associative Echo Weaving:

external signals
→ cross-modal binding
→ memory
→ later reactivation
→ internally experienced thought
→ behavior / new memory

And then our usual experiment applies: vary one channel, remove another, preserve the Golden Reality baseline, and measure what thought-patterns still regenerate independently.

That lets you distinguish what was planted, what was amplified, and what was already yours.